<CaseStudy title="Embedded Workforce Platform Integration" description="A reference architecture for a coherent onboarding experience across partner and employment platforms." category="Solution design / Independent fictional case study" role="Architecture design & technical presentation" scope="Ownership · Orchestration · Events · Recovery" next="/work/security-automation/" nextLabel="Security Workflow Automation">
  <p slot="notice" class="case-notice">Independent architecture exercise using fictional platforms. This is an illustrative design, not an internal or production system. API examples, states, and provider capabilities require validation before implementation.</p>
  <Fragment slot="links"><a class="button" href="/downloads/embedded-workforce-platform-integration.pptx" download>Download full presentation <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><span class="download-detail">PowerPoint · 18 slides with speaker notes</span></Fragment>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The scenario</p><h2>One customer journey.<br />Two systems of record.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>A fictional Partner Platform wants to offer embedded hiring through an Employment Platform. Customers should be able to start a hire and follow its progress inside the partner product, even though the provider controls employment, compliance, payroll, and contracts.</p><p>The design challenge is to make that experience understandable without concealing system boundaries or pretending that a long-running onboarding process is one instant transaction.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / Architecture principles</p><h2>Ownership comes<br />before integration code.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The partner owns tenant context, permissions, workflow, and the customer experience. The employment provider remains authoritative for the employment lifecycle and compliance records.</p><p>A mapping table connects identifiers across systems. The integration keeps durable progress and presents actionable states; it does not infer hidden compliance decisions from transport responses.</p><ul><li><strong>Reliable:</strong> every hire has a visible, tracked state.</li><li><strong>Guided:</strong> the customer sees the next action and its owner.</li><li><strong>Recoverable:</strong> failures have a retry, reconciliation, or escalation path.</li></ul></div></section>
  <Slide number={2} title="Architecture Principles" alt="Architecture principles: partner owns customer experience, orchestration, tenants and embedded UI; provider owns employment lifecycle, compliance, payroll and contracts; shared data has an authoritative owner." />
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Reference architecture</p><h2>A thin adapter.<br />Durable orchestration.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The partner backend calls an integration service that handles validation, transformations, authentication, and retries before reaching the provider API. A state store preserves cross-system identifiers and workflow progress.</p><p>Events flow through webhook verification, deduplication, durable processing, and status publication. Failed events enter a dead-letter queue (DLQ) for investigation and replay. A reconciliation worker closes gaps by retrieving current provider state.</p><p>The local state store is the record of integration progress and mappings. The provider API remains authoritative for the current employment state.</p></div></section>
  <Slide number={4} title="Reference Architecture" alt="Partner UI and backend call an integration service, provider API gateway and employment domain services. Supporting components include a queue and dead-letter queue, webhook receiver, mapping and state store, reconciliation worker, and status publishing back to the partner." />
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Onboarding sequence</p><h2>Commit the mapping<br />before the invitation.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The workflow uses a saga: persist progress between steps so a partial failure can be recovered. It avoids depending on a distributed transaction across systems neither side fully controls.</p><ol><li>Validate the draft and reserve local workflow state, including an idempotency key and correlation ID.</li><li>Create the employment record with the provider.</li><li>Commit the partner-to-provider mapping and mark creation complete.</li><li>Invite the employee, then track subsequent lifecycle events.</li><li>Publish normalized, actionable progress to the partner UI.</li></ol><p>If creation succeeds but saving the mapping fails, the record remains in a recovery state. A retry must resolve the existing provider record rather than blindly creating another hire.</p></div></section>
  <Slide number={6} title="Onboarding Sequence" alt="Sequence diagram: submit draft, validate and reserve saga state, create provider employment, commit identifier mapping, invite employee, process lifecycle webhooks, persist state, and display actionable progress." />
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">05 / Event-driven status updates</p><h2>Fast feedback.<br />A path back to correctness.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Webhooks make changes visible quickly, but delivery can be duplicated, delayed, reordered, or missed. The receiver verifies signatures; the processor checks event identity and ordering before projecting changes into the partner status model.</p><p>Duplicate events are ignored. Ambiguous ordering triggers a lookup of current provider state. Missed changes are recovered through periodic reconciliation. Invalid payloads and unknown mappings go to the DLQ with an operations recovery path.</p><p>The user sees a business state and an action—such as correcting a field or waiting for synchronization—rather than a raw transport error.</p></div></section>
  <Slide number={10} title="Event-Driven Status Updates" alt="Provider webhook passes through signature verification, deduplication and ordering checks, status projection, and partner callback or API. Event IDs, a dead-letter queue with alerting, and a reconciliation worker support the pipeline." />
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">06 / Why this design</p><h2>Make the first workflow<br />safe to expand.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Saga state and reconciliation support recovery across independent systems. Explicit provider authority avoids competing versions of the employment lifecycle. Normalized partner states reduce the UI’s dependence on provider-specific details.</p><p>The proposed delivery starts with embedded onboarding and status visibility, then adds time-off workflows, deeper lifecycle capabilities, and partner-facing operational tools once the first flow is stable.</p><p>Before implementation, I would validate authentication and tenant boundaries, API idempotency, webhook contracts, rate limits, and sandbox failure paths. Those are open questions in the presentation, not assumed provider guarantees.</p></div></section>
  <Slide number={15} title="Why This Design" alt="Design choices are saga and reconciliation, provider authority for employment lifecycle, and normalized partner states. Proposed expansion moves from onboarding to time off, lifecycle workflows, and partner operational tooling." />
